WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.473
A practical secrets review for a Go service starts with inventory.

2
00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:11.502
Identify the values that grant access, prove identity, protect integrity, or unlock private material.

3
00:00:11.502 --> 00:00:21.247
Separate ordinary configuration from true secrets. Name each secret by purpose, owner, environment, permission boundary, and rotation path.

4
00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:29.794
If no one knows who owns a credential or how it can be replaced, the service has an operational risk, not just a documentation gap.

5
00:00:29.794 --> 00:00:32.750
Next, check where secrets enter the system.

6
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:41.377
They should not be hardcoded in source, embedded in binaries, committed to fixtures, baked into Docker layers, or printed by CI.

7
00:00:41.377 --> 00:00:53.997
A Go configuration loader should use explicit required settings, prefer os.LookupEnv when it needs to distinguish unset from empty, and fail closed when production secrets are missing.

8
00:00:53.997 --> 00:01:02.464
Startup errors should name missing configuration keys without printing values. Then inspect how secrets move through code.

9
00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:10.612
Pass them only to components that need them. Avoid broad global config objects that every package can print or serialize.

10
00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:12.848
Redact secret-bearing structs.

11
00:01:12.848 --> 00:01:21.235
Do not log full DSNs, Authorization headers, raw config, provider responses, or panic output containing sensitive fields.

12
00:01:21.235 --> 00:01:29.942
If the service needs diagnostics, log safe identifiers and error classes instead. Provider integration deserves its own check.

13
00:01:29.942 --> 00:01:38.968
If the service uses a managed store, verify authentication, least privilege, context timeouts, retry limits, and audit visibility.

14
00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:47.675
If the platform injects secrets, verify file permissions, environment exposure, refresh behavior, and restart expectations.

15
00:01:47.675 --> 00:01:56.142
If the service caches secrets, document the time-to-live, refresh policy, failure behavior, and revocation implications.

16
00:01:56.142 --> 00:02:03.970
Finally, review rotation, testing, CI/CD, and incident response. Rotation should be possible without guesswork.

17
00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:11.718
Tests should use fake providers and nonworking values unless an integration environment is explicitly required.

18
00:02:11.718 --> 00:02:20.505
CI secrets should be scoped, protected from logs, and separated by environment. Secret scanning should help block risky commits.

19
00:02:20.505 --> 00:02:32.167
Incident response should include how to identify affected secrets, rotate or revoke them, redeploy safely, and confirm that logs and artifacts did not spread the exposure.

20
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:34.962
Secrets management is a system behavior.

21
00:02:34.962 --> 00:02:43.270
Go code matters, but so do runtime identity, infrastructure policy, developer workflow, observability, and operations.

22
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:52.136
A secure service is not one that merely hides a string. It is one that can load, use, rotate, audit, and recover secrets deliberately.
